## 🧩 Anatomy of a Watch Dial

A typical watch dial is composed of several distinct components:

```text
┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                       [ 12 O'Clock ]                      │
│                          Brand Logo                       │
│                                                           │
│    [ Sub-dial ]           [ Hands ]         [ Date Window]│
│                                                           │
│                       Minute Track/Indices                │
│                        [ 6 O'Clock ]                      │
└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```text
[ Battery / Solar Cell ] ──> [ Circuit Board ] ──> [ Quartz Crystal (32,768 Hz) ]
                                                              │
[ Hands on Dial ] <── [ Gear Train ] <── [ Stepping Motor ] <──┘
```

1. **The Power Source:** A battery or rechargeable cell sends a small electrical current through an integrated circuit board.
2. **The Piezoelectric Effect:** The current passes through a tiny, tuning-fork-shaped quartz crystal. Thanks to the *piezoelectric effect*, the quartz crystal vibrates at an ultra-precise frequency of **32,768 times per second (Hz)**.
3. **The Microchip Divider:** An electronic circuit counts these oscillations and converts them into precisely **1 pulse per second**.
4. **The Stepping Motor:** Each pulse triggers a miniature stepping motor, which steps the gear train forward and moves the second hand with that signature, crisp "tick-tick" motion.

Because this entire mechanical system requires constant electrical current to vibrate the crystal and drive the motor, **an energy source is strictly necessary**.

